<article class="env-definition" id="AECA43">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/AECA43">Definition <span data-tag="AECA43">8.2.8.1</span>.</a>cofibration!projectivecofibration!injectivefibration!injectivefibration!projectiveinjective!cofibrationinjective!fibrationprojective!cofibrationprojective!fibration Let $\calC $ be a small category, and $\mathbf{A}$ a model category. A natural transformation $\alpha : F \rightarrow G$ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ is a: </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li><p>
<em>injective cofibration</em> if the induced map $F(C) \rightarrow G(C)$ is a cofibration in $\mathbf{A}$, for each $C \in \calC $. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
<em>projective fibration</em> if the induced map $F(C) \rightarrow G(C)$ is a fibration in $\mathbf{A}$, for each $C \in \calC $. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
<em>weak equivalence</em> if the induced map $F(C) \rightarrow G(C)$ is a weak equivalence in $\mathbf{A}$, for each $C \in \calC $. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
<em>injective fibration</em> if it has the right lifting property with respect to every morphism $\beta $ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ which is simultaneously a weak equivalence and a injective cofibration. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
<em>projective cofibration</em> if it has the left lifting property with respect to every morphism $\beta $ in $\Fun (\calC , \mathbf{A})$ which is simultaneously a weak equivalence and a projective fibration. 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>